  "headline": "Oil cracks, my gold line broke, and the thesis survives only in the curve",
  "body": [
    "Today is a growth scare, not a boom and not a financing squeeze. Brent lost close to 7% in a session while the S&P 500 fell 0.5%, and the VIX jumped to 21, up 38% on the month. Yields fell across the curve with the front end leading, and gold found a bid for the first time in weeks, up 1.7% to $3,951. The oil tax is being refunded and the market is spending the refund on bonds and gold, not on stocks.",
    "Last week I set a line: gold breaking its 52-week low near $3,960 with the dollar still falling would mean the thesis was broken, not early. That happened. The low is now $3,885 and the dollar is down 0.3% on the week, so I hold myself to it: today's bounce is a trade off a floor, not a reinstatement. My book was also wrong on Brent and copper and sat out the long bond's rally, which is the price of a standing view.",
    "What survives is the curve. The 5-year fell 13 basis points this week while the 30-year fell only 3, so the 30-5 spread widened to 58 basis points with stocks falling. That is the shape I said would bring the thesis back: the market pricing easier policy while the long end refuses to follow, and MOVE at 100, three-quarters of the way up its range, says bonds are watchful rather than breaking. Bitcoin is no help to me: down 9% on the month and buying 4% fewer ounces of gold even as gold fell, it is trading as a risk asset, not a liquidity hedge.",
    "Drop Site News reports that a consultancy is helping the Pentagon plan the Iran war's next phase, and that a UN staffer died in Ansarallah detention. Neither story is in today's tape, because a market pricing escalation does not take 7% off crude in a day. The market is pricing supply relief or demand loss and I cannot tell which from here; Brent's realized volatility at three-quarters of its own year's median says the drop was orderly, not forced. The importers get the benefit, with the rupee up 1.5% against the dollar over the month.",
    "I cut the Brent long; I do not own crude into a growth scare after a seven-dollar day. I keep copper lightly at 78% of its range. I add gold as a trade off $3,885, the first bid into dollar weakness I have seen in a month, and I still avoid the 30-year at 5.27% because it will not rally with the 5-year. I drop my preference for the loonie over the rupee while oil's premium bleeds. Gold through $3,885 proves me wrong on the trade; the 30-5 spread closing back under 50 basis points as the long end catches up proves me wrong on the bond. Next I am watching whether Brent follows through below $100."
  ],
